# Expectation record for 14_abi_calling_convention/002_many_float_parameters.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 002_many_float_parameters
area               = 14_abi_calling_convention
description        = Fourteen floating parameters plus a trailing integer tag per call, double and float, sized to exhaust the floating argument registers of every supported ABI; folded and volatile-runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour by construction, which is the precondition that
makes a divergence here evidence about the compiler rather than about the test.

WHAT IS PASSED, AND WHY THE COUNT IS FOURTEEN. Each of the two callees takes fourteen floating
parameters plus a trailing int tag, and each is called twice -- once over literal arguments and
once over arguments staged out of volatile storage. The widest floating argument file across the
four supported ABIs is eight (System V AMD64 xmm0-xmm7, AAPCS64 v0-v7, LP64D fa0-fa7), and
System V i386 cdecl has no argument registers at all, so fourteen clears eight by six and no
target can satisfy either call from its floating registers alone. Every one of the fourteen
parameters is printed back individually and in declaration order, never summed and never
checksummed, so a transposed pair or a single mishandled slot names itself instead of
cancelling out. The fifteenth parameter is the int tag; it is consumed through variant_tag and
appears as the variant name that every line of a group carries, so a tag delivered to the wrong
slot renames a whole group and is just as visible as a wrong value would be.

- No arithmetic can overflow, trap or lose a value, because there is almost none: beyond the one
  equality comparison inside variant_tag and the two loop counters that drive the staging
  copies, every floating value is copied from a literal or from an initialised volatile object
  into a parameter and then printed. So there is no signed integer overflow, no division, no
  remainder, no shift of any kind, and no operation that could produce a not-a-number or an
  infinity.
- Every literal is a dyadic rational with a denominator of at most 16, hence exactly
  representable in binary32 and in binary64 alike. No conversion in this program loses a value
  and %.4f never meets a rounding tie.
- Each float is widened to double by an explicit cast at the printf call site. The widening is
  exact in every case because binary32 is a subset of binary64, and writing the conversion out
  rather than leaving it to the default argument promotion is also what keeps the program clean
  under the audit gate's -Wconversion, from which this area sanctions no deviation.
- No index is out of range and no pointer past the end of an object is formed. Both staging
  loops run from 0 to 13 over arrays of fourteen elements. No pointer arithmetic is performed
  at all; the only pointers in the program are the format strings and the const char * returned
  by variant_tag, which points into a string literal of static storage duration and therefore
  stays valid for the lifetime of the program.
- No read of uninitialised storage. Every element of both volatile arrays and the volatile tag
  are initialised in their own declarations and written nowhere afterwards, and every plain
  staging destination is written by its loop or its assignment before it is ever read.
- No aliasing violation: every object is read through its own declared type, and no storage is
  reinterpreted as another type.
- No object is modified twice between sequence points, no argument to any call has a side
  effect, and nothing depends on padding bytes or on the relative addresses of unrelated
  objects.

NOTHING DEPENDS ON UNSPECIFIED EVALUATION ORDER, and that is enforced structurally rather than
merely argued. An access to a volatile object is an observable side effect, and C11 6.5.2.2p10
leaves the order of evaluation of a call's function designator, of its arguments, and of
subexpressions within those arguments unspecified, so reading fourteen volatile elements inside
one argument list would make the order of fourteen side effects unspecified. The runtime
variants therefore STAGE every volatile datum before any call: the tag is copied into a plain
int in a statement of its own, and each element of vdbl and vflt is copied into plain array
storage in a bounded loop, one volatile read per iteration and so one per full expression. The
calls then pass plain objects only, and neither argument list contains a side effect at all.
Staging costs the test nothing, which is why it is the right answer rather than a compromise: a
value that arrived through a volatile load stays opaque to the optimizer, so every argument is
still materialised at run time and still marshalled through the ABI.

THE CALL BOUNDARY IS ENFORCED RATHER THAN HOPED FOR, and in pure ISO C. Both callees are reached
through a volatile-qualified function pointer instead of by name, because a fixed-arity static
callee may legally be inlined at -O1 and -O2 and an inlined callee marshals nothing at all --
the floating argument path this program exists to exercise would then never be crossed, and the
register assignments argued above would be claims about code that was never emitted. A volatile
pointer must be re-read at the point of call, so the designated function is unknown and the call
is genuinely indirect; and because the address escapes into storage, the signature may not be
cloned or scalarised either. An inlining attribute would have been shorter, but the documented
attribute set for the compiler under test is packed, aligned, section, unused, deprecated,
visibility and format (docs/technical-specifications.md line 506), so an attribute would risk a
divergence caused by the test and would import an extension into an area whose subject is the
calling convention.

No header is included. bcc ships no stdio.h -- its bundled set is the nine required freestanding
headers plus a bonus stdatomic.h (docs/technical-specifications.md line 19 and lines 202-214) --
so an include would fail against bcc while succeeding against the reference compiler,
manufacturing a divergence caused by the test rather than by the compiler. printf is therefore
hand-declared, and float.h is not included either, no macro from it being needed. Area 14 takes
neither of the suite's two sanctioned header exceptions. main returns 0, inside the permitted
0-125 range. Nothing is allocated; no socket, file, environment variable, clock or random source
is touched; and every input is a literal in the source.

Verified empirically on this branch with gcc 13.4.0 and qemu-user 10.1.0: the default seven-flag
warning gate (-Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror) produces
zero diagnostics at -O0, -O1 and -O2; the sanitizer gate (-fsanitize=undefined,address
-fno-sanitize-recover=all) runs to completion with empty output and exit status 0; and all twelve
reference cells of the declared matrix produce byte-identical stdout with exit status 0 and an
empty standard error.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This area exists because the four backends implement four different application binary
interfaces, and it is the densest concentration of cross-backend divergence surface in the
corpus. This program's subject is the FLOATING argument register file specifically, and its
parameter count is sized against the widest such file rather than the narrowest: eight on System
V AMD64 (xmm0-xmm7), AAPCS64 (v0-v7) and LP64D (fa0-fa7), and none at all on System V i386
cdecl, which marshals every argument on the stack. Fourteen floating parameters therefore clear
the widest file by six on every target, so no target is left exercising only its register path.
Where the six excess values go was read out of the reference compiler's own assembly for this
exact program rather than assumed, and checked against the documented ABI rows at
docs/technical-specifications.md line 546 (x86-64), line 553 (i686 cdecl), line 559 (AAPCS64)
and line 565 (LP64D):

  x86-64   System V AMD64  eight in xmm0-xmm7  six on the stack      tag in edi
  i686     System V cdecl  no argument registers at all, all fifteen on the stack
  AArch64  AAPCS64         eight in d0-d7      six on the stack      tag in w0
  RISC-V   LP64D           eight in fa0-fa7    six in INTEGER a0-a5  tag in a6

The RISC-V row is why the trailing tag must stay last. Three of the four ABIs spill the excess
floating values to the stack; LP64D instead passes a floating argument that no longer fits the
floating file in an INTEGER argument register before resorting to the stack, so six doubles
cross into a0-a5 as raw bit patterns and the tag is pushed along to a6. The two register files
are therefore deliberately COUPLED there and independent on x86-64 and AAPCS64, where the tag
takes the first integer register while the floating file is already full. One trailing parameter
thus discriminates between two opposite allocator errors on three targets and exercises the
plain stack path on the fourth.

All of that is a difference between the calling conventions, and the calling conventions
differing is exactly what is under test. The OBSERVABLE RESULTS must nevertheless agree, so this
record carries no target restriction, no oracle exclusion, no warning-gate deviation and no
expected-divergence marker: a cross-backend difference here is a genuine finding rather than an
implementation-defined one. The measured target differences that could otherwise reach the
output are handled by construction instead:

1. Width normalisation. sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) are 4 on i686 and 8 on x86_64, aarch64
   and riscv64. The only types this program passes are double, float and int, and the only
   types it prints are double and const char *; no long, no long long, no size_t and no pointer
   value appears anywhere in the output.
2. Plain char signedness is implementation-defined and was measured signed on x86_64 and i686
   and unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64. No plain char is passed or printed: char occurs only as
   const char *, for the format strings and for the variant name.
3. long double is deliberately absent. Its representation was measured to differ across the four
   targets (16, 12, 16 and 16 bytes; x87 80-bit extended versus IEEE binary128), so using it
   here would import a divergence that has nothing to do with argument passing. That type
   belongs to 13_floating_point/004_long_double_target_restricted, which keeps it under test
   with a recorded oracle (b) exclusion carrying its measured reason.
4. Floating values are printed at fixed precision with margin, and every literal is a dyadic
   rational with a denominator of at most 16, so each is exactly representable in both binary32
   and binary64 and prints exactly at four decimal places with no rounding tie. That is what
   keeps the output byte-identical even on i686, whose reference compiler evaluates with x87
   80-bit intermediates (FLT_EVAL_METHOD == 2), where double rounding would otherwise be the
   hazard.
5. The format set is exactly %s and %.4f. Every float reaching the variadic printf carries an
   explicit (double) cast, so each conversion specification matches its argument's type after
   the default argument promotions; %d, %ld, %lu, %zu and %p never appear.
6. No aggregate is declared, passed or returned, so no aggregate size, alignment or padding
   difference between the targets can affect this program at all, and no member is read back.
   Aggregate argument passing belongs to 004_small_and_large_struct_passing and aggregate
   return to 005_struct_return_by_value.
7. All four targets are little-endian and no byte-order-sensitive value is printed. sizeof is
   never printed, no object is compared byte-wise, and no address or pointer value is printed:
   the program observes parameter VALUES and nothing else.

Cross-target identity is therefore engineered by construction and then measured, not assumed.
The golden record below was captured on this branch with gcc 13.4.0 and confirmed byte-identical
across all twelve reference cells -- x86_64 natively and i686, aarch64 and riscv64 under
qemu-user 10.1.0, each at -O0, -O1 and -O2 -- every one exiting 0 with an empty standard error.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
